# Nothing in this file is required to run the build. Every one of these gates a
# feature that says out loud, on the page, that it is not configured.
#
# KEVIN SETS THESE IN THE VERCEL DASHBOARD. Never paste a secret into a file in
# this repo, and never ask for one in a chat.

# --- Jelly ordering -------------------------------------------------------
#
# The database. One click in Vercel: project > Storage > Create Database >
# Neon, free tier. Tables create themselves on first use.
#
# WITHOUT THIS, ORDERING RUNS ON IN-MEMORY STORAGE and the kitchen screen can
# miss orders that landed on a different lambda. The screen says so in a red
# box rather than half-working quietly. Set it before any demo with two
# devices, and certainly before any real order.
#
# In production, /kitchen sign-in also refuses until this is set: the login
# throttle counts guesses in Postgres or not at all, because a per-instance
# memory count resets on every deploy and can be jammed by one address flood.
# The sign-in form answers 503 and names this variable. Development counts in
# memory and needs nothing.
# DATABASE_URL=

# The counter's screen at /kitchen. Falls back to 0105 (their street number),
# which is a PLACEHOLDER and is in the repo, so set a real one before staff
# use it.
# KITCHEN_PIN=

# Signs the counter's session cookie. 32 characters or more, random, never
# reused from another project. REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION: without it the site
# refuses to sign anyone in at /kitchen and says so on the form. Changing it
# signs every open session out, which is the intended way to do that.
# KITCHEN_SESSION_SECRET=

# Sign-in throttling counts guesses per connecting address, so the server must
# be able to read that address. On Vercel, the project setting "Automatically
# expose System Environment Variables" must be ON: it gives the server VERCEL=1,
# and the server then reads the x-vercel-forwarded-for header Vercel writes at
# its edge. Leave this variable unset on Vercel. On any other host, set it to
# the header a trusted proxy overwrites (x-forwarded-for, x-real-ip or
# x-vercel-forwarded-for) and block direct access to the app. When no trusted
# address can be read, sign-in answers 503 with reason trusted_address_unavailable
# instead of guessing. Local loopback in development needs nothing here.
# KITCHEN_TRUSTED_IP_HEADER=

# Forces the ordering window open regardless of the clock, so the system can be
# shown at 9pm on a Sunday. REMOVE THIS AT GO-LIVE: left on, it takes orders
# for counters that are dark.
# ORDERING_DEMO_ALWAYS_OPEN=1

# Thermal printers, comma separated, as id:role:location.
# role is kitchen or front. location is marshall or battle-creek, and omitting
# it means that printer takes both counters' tickets.
# No printers configured is a supported state: the kitchen screen carries.
# ORDERING_PRINTERS=mar-kitchen:kitchen:marshall,mar-front:front:marshall

# --- payments -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# UNSET MEANS DEMO MODE. The order is accepted, no card is taken, and both the
# checkout and the printed slip say so. Setting this switches the order route's
# marked payment seam to a real Stripe Checkout session on the deli's connected
# account, with the 99 cent fee split via application_fee_amount.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# STRIPE_CONNECTED_ACCOUNT=

# --- mail -----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The guest's order confirmation. Unset logs the whole payload instead, which
# is the honest unconfigured state: the delivery is what is missing, and that
# is the operator's problem to see in the log rather than the visitor's problem
# to see in a red box. An email failure never fails an order.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# ORDER_FROM=orders@glazedweb.com
# ORDER_REPLY_TO=
